Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport:

---

## Program.cs

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is reserved for JSON-RPC protocol
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("WeatherApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weather.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## WeatherTools.cs

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current weather for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetCurrentWeather(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/current?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get 5-day forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        [Description("Number of days (1-5)")] int days = 5,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        return await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&days={days}", cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates with the standard .NET DI container (`Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`):

| Injection Style | How It Works |
|----------------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Use a non-static class with a primary constructor. Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. are resolved when the tool class is instantiated. |
| **Method parameter injection** | Any parameter whose type is registered in DI (or is `CancellationToken`/`IMcpServer`/`IProgress<>`) is resolved automatically and **hidden from the tool schema**. |

**Key points:**
- Only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the JSON schema that LLMs see
- `CancellationToken`, `ILogger<T>`, `IHttpClientFactory`, `IMcpServer`, and `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` are all injected — never exposed to the LLM
- Register services in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient()`, `AddSingleton<>()`, etc.) before calling `AddMcpServer()`
- `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection

---

To scaffold this yourself:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will start and wait for JSON-RPC messages on stdin. Connect it from VS Code, GitHub Copilot, or any MCP client by configuring it as a stdio subprocess.
